1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,620
The police can search your car without a

2
00:00:01,620 --> 00:00:01,880
warrant.

3
00:00:02,520 --> 00:00:04,600
They've been able to for a hundred years —

4
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:06,140
with one exception.

5
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:07,540
You get pulled over.

6
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:11,340
The officer walks up, glances through the

7
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:17,620
glass, and says he's going to search the

8
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:17,880
car.

9
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:18,580
Can he?

10
00:00:18,580 --> 00:00:18,820
When?

11
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:21,780
And where, exactly, does that power run out?

12
00:00:21,780 --> 00:00:23,900
The answer stretches from a Prohibition

13
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:27,040
bootlegger on a Michigan highway to a

14
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:29,440
motorcycle hidden under a tarp in a Virginia

15
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:29,600
driveway —

16
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:32,240
and it quietly decides how much privacy you

17
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:36,780
actually keep the moment you turn the key.

18
00:00:36,780 --> 00:00:39,100
Because there is one place they still can't

19
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:39,280
go —

20
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:43,800
and in 2018 the Supreme Court drew that

21
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:47,580
line at the very edge of your home.

22
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:51,900
Start with the thing that surprises almost

23
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:52,100
everyone.

24
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:55,600
To search your house, the police usually

25
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:56,720
need a warrant —

26
00:00:56,720 --> 00:00:59,480
a judge's signature, probable cause written

27
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:03,620
down, a name for the place and the

28
00:01:03,620 --> 00:01:04,800
thing they're after.

29
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:06,660
That is the Fourth Amendment at full

30
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:07,620
strength.

31
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:09,140
Your car does not get that.

32
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:10,880
And it hasn't since 1925 —

33
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:12,020
a hundred years ago.

34
00:01:12,020 --> 00:01:13,060
It was Prohibition.

35
00:01:13,060 --> 00:01:16,020
Alcohol was illegal, and a whole underground

36
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:18,440
economy ran it across state lines by car.

37
00:01:18,440 --> 00:01:21,140
A man named George Carroll was one of

38
00:01:21,140 --> 00:01:23,140
those runners, moving liquor along the

39
00:01:23,140 --> 00:01:25,360
highway between Detroit and Grand Rapids.

40
00:01:25,360 --> 00:01:27,280
Federal agents had been watching him.

41
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:30,880
They stopped his car, and they didn't find

42
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:32,040
anything in plain sight —

43
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:34,500
so they started tearing into it.

44
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:36,500
They ripped open the seat cushions.

45
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:37,620
And hidden behind the upholstery of the

46
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:39,480
seats, they pulled out sixty-eight bottles

47
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:40,520
of whisky and gin.

48
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:42,660
Carroll's lawyers had what sounds like a

49
00:01:42,660 --> 00:01:44,120
winning argument: no warrant, no search.

50
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:47,020
The agents had torn his car apart on

51
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:53,100
the side of the road with nothing on

52
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:53,400
paper.

53
00:01:53,560 --> 00:01:57,020
The case climbed all the way to the

54
00:01:57,020 --> 00:01:57,300
Supreme Court.

55
00:01:57,300 --> 00:01:59,340
And the Court gave an answer that has

56
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:00,980
governed your glovebox ever since.

57
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:02,740
It drew a line —

58
00:02:02,740 --> 00:02:04,780
not around what the police wanted,

59
00:02:04,780 --> 00:02:06,820
but around the thing being searched.

60
00:02:06,820 --> 00:02:07,900
A house, the Court said,

61
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:08,900
stays put.

62
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:12,620
If officers have to leave and get a

63
00:02:12,620 --> 00:02:14,880
warrant, they can post someone at the door;

64
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:16,900
the house isn't going anywhere.

65
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:18,580
But a car is built to move.

66
00:02:18,580 --> 00:02:22,920
By the time a warrant is signed,

67
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:23,300
a car —

68
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:25,620
and whatever is hidden in its seats —

69
00:02:25,620 --> 00:02:28,160
can be in another county,

70
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:29,100
another state, gone.

71
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:31,660
Chief Justice Taft wrote that you can't

72
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:33,600
treat the two the same,

73
00:02:34,380 --> 00:02:36,700
"because the vehicle can be quickly moved

74
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:39,660
out of the locality or jurisdiction in which

75
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:43,160
the warrant must be sought."

76
00:02:43,160 --> 00:02:46,360
So the Court carved out what we now

77
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:48,000
call the automobile exception.

78
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,780
If police have probable cause to believe

79
00:02:51,780 --> 00:02:53,720
your car contains contraband, they can

80
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:54,060
search it —

81
00:02:54,060 --> 00:02:55,500
right there, no warrant.

82
00:02:55,500 --> 00:03:00,060
And this isn't a dusty relic.

83
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:03,160
That 1925 rule is still the rule behind

84
00:03:03,160 --> 00:03:04,620
why an ordinary traffic stop can,

85
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:07,040
under the right facts, become an open trunk

86
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:09,440
on the shoulder of the highway.

87
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:11,360
Not everyone agreed at the time.

88
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:12,840
Justice McReynolds, joined by Justice

89
00:03:12,840 --> 00:03:13,180
Sutherland, dissented.

90
00:03:13,180 --> 00:03:14,680
But the majority held —

91
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:17,560
and here's the phrase to hold onto,

92
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:21,060
because the entire story turns on it.

93
00:03:21,060 --> 00:03:21,640
Probable cause.

94
00:03:21,640 --> 00:03:23,540
Not a warrant.

95
00:03:23,540 --> 00:03:24,120
Not certainty.

96
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:27,560
But not a hunch either.

97
00:03:27,560 --> 00:03:30,060
We're going to see exactly where they bite.

98
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:32,860
Here's the part movies and TV get wrong.

99
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:35,280
The automobile exception is not a blank

100
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:35,500
check.

101
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:37,520
Being pulled over is not,

102
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:39,000
by itself, permission to search your car.

103
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:42,860
And a hunch is not enough.

104
00:03:42,860 --> 00:03:44,560
Probable cause means a fair,

105
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:46,440
fact-based probability that there's

106
00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:47,920
contraband or evidence inside —

107
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:50,260
something an officer can point to and

108
00:03:50,260 --> 00:03:52,380
articulate, not just a feeling.

109
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:53,480
A busted taillight does not,

110
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:55,260
on its own, get anyone into your trunk.

111
00:03:55,260 --> 00:03:57,380
So what does clear the bar?

112
00:03:57,880 --> 00:03:59,380
The smell of something illegal in some

113
00:03:59,380 --> 00:03:59,600
places.

114
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:01,880
Something an officer sees in plain view on

115
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:02,600
the seat.

116
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:03,640
An admission.

117
00:04:04,360 --> 00:04:05,240
A trained dog's alert.

118
00:04:05,240 --> 00:04:06,440
Facts that add up.

119
00:04:06,440 --> 00:04:08,540
And once that line is crossed —

120
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:11,480
once there really is probable cause —

121
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:14,040
the search can go much further than people

122
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:14,280
expect.

123
00:04:14,280 --> 00:04:16,720
Because the Supreme Court has said the reach

124
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:19,540
of the search is defined by the thing

125
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:20,840
they're looking for.

126
00:04:21,420 --> 00:04:24,900
If the object could fit in the glovebox,

127
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:26,020
they can open the glovebox.

128
00:04:26,020 --> 00:04:28,000
If it could be in the trunk,

129
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,940
they can open the trunk.

130
00:04:30,940 --> 00:04:34,160
If it could be inside a zipped bag

131
00:04:34,160 --> 00:04:36,760
on the back seat, they can open the

132
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:36,940
bag.

133
00:04:36,940 --> 00:04:38,180
Locked container, loose container —

134
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:41,380
if that's a place the thing could be,

135
00:04:41,380 --> 00:04:41,960
it's fair game.

136
00:04:41,960 --> 00:04:44,240
That reach didn't come out of nowhere.

137
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:46,940
It was settled in two Supreme Court

138
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:47,120
decisions —

139
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:48,440
United States v.

140
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:50,660
Ross in 1982, and California v.

141
00:04:50,660 --> 00:04:51,740
Acevedo in 1991 —

142
00:04:52,220 --> 00:04:54,220
which held that a closed bag or a

143
00:04:54,220 --> 00:04:57,420
locked box inside the car gets no special

144
00:04:57,420 --> 00:04:58,020
shield of its own.

145
00:04:58,020 --> 00:05:00,280
If the thing they have probable cause to

146
00:05:00,280 --> 00:05:02,120
find could be in there,

147
00:05:02,120 --> 00:05:03,220
they can open it.

148
00:05:03,340 --> 00:05:04,920
But that same rule cuts the other way,

149
00:05:04,920 --> 00:05:10,640
and it's the closest thing you have to

150
00:05:12,460 --> 00:05:12,720
a leash.

151
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:16,880
The search has to fit the reason for

152
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:17,280
it.

153
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:23,160
If the probable cause is about a stolen

154
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:25,580
fifty-inch television, that does not justify

155
00:05:25,580 --> 00:05:26,400
opening a tiny pill bottle —

156
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:28,280
a TV can't hide in there.

157
00:05:28,280 --> 00:05:29,960
The bigger the thing they're chasing,

158
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:32,860
the fewer the places they're allowed to

159
00:05:32,860 --> 00:05:33,160
look.

160
00:05:33,160 --> 00:05:36,900
The smaller it is, the more of your

161
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:38,100
car opens up.

162
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:40,340
They don't even have to prove a separate

163
00:05:40,340 --> 00:05:41,600
emergency, though.

164
00:05:41,940 --> 00:05:44,500
The Court has been blunt about it: if

165
00:05:44,500 --> 00:05:47,180
the car is drivable and there's probable

166
00:05:47,180 --> 00:05:48,040
cause, that's enough —

167
00:05:48,040 --> 00:05:49,520
no extra excuse required.

168
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:53,760
The Justices spelled that out again in the

169
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:55,260
nineteen-nineties: a car that's readily

170
00:05:55,260 --> 00:05:56,580
mobile, plus probable cause, needs nothing

171
00:05:56,580 --> 00:05:56,820
more.

172
00:05:56,820 --> 00:05:58,000
No ticking clock.

173
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,440
No proof the driver was about to speed

174
00:06:00,440 --> 00:06:00,660
off.

175
00:06:00,660 --> 00:06:03,600
The car being a car is the emergency.

176
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:06,660
Now step back and feel what that means

177
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:07,600
for you.

178
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:09,500
Your car is where you keep your bag,

179
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:11,840
your phone, your paperwork, half of your

180
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:13,980
private life while it's in motion.

181
00:06:13,980 --> 00:06:15,880
And it carries far thinner protection than

182
00:06:15,880 --> 00:06:16,360
your living room —

183
00:06:16,360 --> 00:06:19,340
for a reason written down before your

184
00:06:19,340 --> 00:06:19,860
grandparents were born.

185
00:06:19,860 --> 00:06:22,460
Which raises the only question that's left.

186
00:06:22,460 --> 00:06:25,200
If the car is this exposed —

187
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:27,920
is there anywhere the police still cannot

188
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:28,260
follow it?

189
00:06:28,260 --> 00:06:31,240
To find the line, go to Virginia —

190
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:33,060
and a motorcycle that kept getting away.

191
00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:35,520
An officer had twice been outrun by the

192
00:06:35,520 --> 00:06:38,620
same distinctive orange-and-black bike, and

193
00:06:39,380 --> 00:06:41,140
he suspected it was stolen.

194
00:06:41,140 --> 00:06:45,100
Then, one day, he tracked it down.

195
00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:46,240
Not on a street.

196
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:47,660
To a house.

197
00:06:47,660 --> 00:06:50,460
And there it was: parked in the driveway,

198
00:06:50,460 --> 00:06:52,480
pulled up close to the side of the

199
00:06:52,480 --> 00:06:55,180
home, with a tarp thrown over it.

200
00:06:55,180 --> 00:07:01,460
So the officer walked up the driveway.

201
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:02,660
He lifted the tarp.

202
00:07:02,660 --> 00:07:06,960
He ran the plate and the vehicle number —

203
00:07:07,600 --> 00:07:09,600
and it came back stolen.

204
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:11,800
Then he waited for the man staying there,

205
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:14,560
Ryan Collins, and arrested him.

206
00:07:14,560 --> 00:07:18,000
Here's why this case is the whole ballgame.

207
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:21,000
The officer had real reason to think that

208
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:21,840
bike was stolen —

209
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:23,140
that's probable cause.

210
00:07:23,140 --> 00:07:27,300
And if that motorcycle had been sitting on

211
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:31,760
a public street, lifting the tarp is a

212
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:32,820
clean, legal, automobile-exception search.

213
00:07:33,320 --> 00:07:34,900
So Virginia argued: same thing here.

214
00:07:34,900 --> 00:07:36,220
A vehicle, probable cause, done —

215
00:07:36,220 --> 00:07:40,440
it shouldn't matter that it was in a

216
00:07:40,440 --> 00:07:40,640
driveway.

217
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:44,420
But think about how much that position was

218
00:07:44,420 --> 00:07:46,780
really asking for.

219
00:07:46,780 --> 00:07:49,960
To search a car parked at a stranger's

220
00:07:49,960 --> 00:07:52,260
public curb, an officer needs nothing but

221
00:07:52,260 --> 00:07:52,700
probable cause.

222
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:56,120
Virginia wanted that same power to include

223
00:07:56,120 --> 00:07:57,580
walking onto private property —

224
00:07:57,580 --> 00:08:01,260
up the driveway, past the point where a

225
00:08:01,260 --> 00:08:02,420
home begins.

226
00:08:02,420 --> 00:08:06,960
If that were allowed, the most exposed thing

227
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:09,040
you own, your car, could become the key

228
00:08:09,160 --> 00:08:12,320
that unlocks the most protected place you

229
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:14,720
have, your home.

230
00:08:15,340 --> 00:08:16,960
The Supreme Court said no.

231
00:08:16,960 --> 00:08:19,660
In 2018, by a vote of eight to

232
00:08:19,660 --> 00:08:22,320
one, the Court held that the car-search

233
00:08:22,320 --> 00:08:27,440
power does not reach onto the ground that

234
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:28,920
belongs to your home.

235
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:30,740
There's a word for that ground.

236
00:08:30,740 --> 00:08:32,600
The porch, the front steps,

237
00:08:33,560 --> 00:08:36,120
the strip of driveway pressed against the

238
00:08:36,120 --> 00:08:36,320
walls —

239
00:08:37,260 --> 00:08:40,160
the law calls it the curtilage.

240
00:08:40,160 --> 00:08:44,420
And curtilage is treated as part of the

241
00:08:44,420 --> 00:08:44,860
home.

242
00:08:44,860 --> 00:08:47,380
It gets the home's full protection.

243
00:08:47,380 --> 00:08:50,180
And that isn't a technicality.

244
00:08:50,180 --> 00:08:52,520
The home is the ground the Fourth Amendment

245
00:08:52,520 --> 00:08:53,320
guards most fiercely.

246
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:56,400
The Court's point was simple: you cannot let

247
00:08:56,400 --> 00:09:00,240
a rule built for the open road quietly

248
00:09:00,240 --> 00:09:03,760
swallow the most protected ground a person

249
00:09:03,760 --> 00:09:04,060
owns.

250
00:09:04,060 --> 00:09:06,600
Where you park does not shrink your home.

251
00:09:06,600 --> 00:09:07,920
Your home enlarges what it protects.

252
00:09:10,400 --> 00:09:12,600
Justice Sotomayor, writing for the Court,

253
00:09:12,600 --> 00:09:17,620
said it in one line worth keeping: "The

254
00:09:17,620 --> 00:09:19,460
scope of the automobile exception extends no

255
00:09:19,460 --> 00:09:21,180
further than the automobile itself."

256
00:09:21,180 --> 00:09:22,920
So both things are true at once.

257
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:25,680
Out on the street, that same search is

258
00:09:25,680 --> 00:09:26,080
fine.

259
00:09:26,080 --> 00:09:30,220
But the instant the car is tucked into

260
00:09:30,220 --> 00:09:32,960
the protected ground hugging your house,

261
00:09:34,800 --> 00:09:36,600
the hundred-year exception runs out of road.

262
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:39,220
To step onto that ground and search,

263
00:09:39,220 --> 00:09:40,580
police need a warrant —

264
00:09:40,580 --> 00:09:42,660
or a completely different exception.

265
00:09:42,660 --> 00:09:43,540
Only Justice Alito dissented —

266
00:09:43,540 --> 00:09:47,840
worried the Court was letting a technicality

267
00:09:47,840 --> 00:09:50,740
shield a bike he had strong reason to

268
00:09:50,880 --> 00:09:51,560
believe was stolen.

269
00:09:51,560 --> 00:09:53,100
But he stood alone.

270
00:09:53,100 --> 00:09:54,400
The line held.

271
00:09:54,400 --> 00:09:56,240
So now you know where the line falls.

272
00:09:56,240 --> 00:09:58,540
The question that actually matters is what

273
00:09:58,540 --> 00:10:01,840
it means the next time it's your window

274
00:10:01,840 --> 00:10:04,640
the officer is walking up to.

275
00:10:04,640 --> 00:10:08,660
So here's the actual map of your rights

276
00:10:08,660 --> 00:10:10,520
on the road —

277
00:10:10,520 --> 00:10:12,360
the one almost nobody is handed.

278
00:10:12,360 --> 00:10:15,480
On a public street or a routine stop,

279
00:10:16,780 --> 00:10:20,080
your car can be searched without a warrant —

280
00:10:20,080 --> 00:10:22,080
but only with probable cause,

281
00:10:23,480 --> 00:10:25,640
and only as far as the thing they're

282
00:10:25,640 --> 00:10:28,360
looking for could actually be.

283
00:10:28,360 --> 00:10:29,020
Being arrested doesn't automatically open

284
00:10:29,820 --> 00:10:30,420
the car either.

285
00:10:30,580 --> 00:10:32,160
In a case called Gant,

286
00:10:32,160 --> 00:10:33,980
the Court said that once you're handcuffed

287
00:10:33,980 --> 00:10:36,560
and set away from the vehicle,

288
00:10:36,560 --> 00:10:38,360
officers generally can't search it just

289
00:10:38,360 --> 00:10:39,420
because they arrested you —

290
00:10:39,420 --> 00:10:42,560
not unless it's reasonable to think evidence

291
00:10:42,560 --> 00:10:44,680
of that specific offense is inside.

292
00:10:45,720 --> 00:10:48,380
The protection can even follow you into a

293
00:10:48,380 --> 00:10:48,640
rental.

294
00:10:48,640 --> 00:10:51,040
The Court has said that if you're lawfully

295
00:10:51,040 --> 00:10:52,260
driving a rental car, you can keep a

296
00:10:52,261 --> 00:10:52,761
real expectation of privacy in it —

297
00:10:52,762 --> 00:10:53,262
even if your name isn't on the paperwork.

298
00:10:53,263 --> 00:10:53,763
And there's one honest gray area worth

299
00:10:53,764 --> 00:10:54,264
naming.

300
00:10:54,265 --> 00:10:54,765
For years, in many places,

301
00:10:54,766 --> 00:10:55,266
the smell of marijuana alone was treated as

302
00:10:55,267 --> 00:10:55,767
probable cause.

303
00:10:55,768 --> 00:10:56,268
But the Supreme Court has never set a

304
00:10:56,269 --> 00:10:56,769
single nationwide rule on that —

305
00:10:56,770 --> 00:10:57,270
and in states that have legalized or

306
00:10:57,271 --> 00:10:57,771
decriminalized it, several courts now say

307
00:10:57,772 --> 00:10:58,272
the odor by itself isn't enough anymore.

308
00:10:58,273 --> 00:10:58,773
On that one, the honest answer is: it

309
00:10:58,774 --> 00:10:59,274
depends where you are.

310
00:10:59,275 --> 00:10:59,775
So what does all of this mean the

311
00:10:59,776 --> 00:11:00,276
next time you're actually pulled over?

312
00:11:00,277 --> 00:11:00,777
You don't have to be a lawyer.

313
00:11:00,778 --> 00:11:01,278
Just carry the shape of it: they need

314
00:11:01,279 --> 00:11:01,779
a real, articulable reason —

315
00:11:01,780 --> 00:11:02,280
not a mood —

316
00:11:02,281 --> 00:11:02,781
before they can search; the search has to

317
00:11:02,782 --> 00:11:03,282
match the thing they're after; and the

318
00:11:03,283 --> 00:11:03,783
ground pressed against your house is not the

319
00:11:03,784 --> 00:11:04,284
road.

320
00:11:04,285 --> 00:11:04,785
But the throughline is a century old,

321
00:11:04,786 --> 00:11:05,286
and it's simple.

322
00:11:05,287 --> 00:11:05,787
Your car trades privacy for mobility.

323
00:11:05,788 --> 00:11:06,288
The exception is broad —

324
00:11:06,289 --> 00:11:06,789
broader than most people believe —

325
00:11:06,790 --> 00:11:07,290
and it isn't a myth.

326
00:11:07,291 --> 00:11:07,791
It's just not infinite.

327
00:11:07,792 --> 00:11:08,292
It stops, exactly, at the edge of your

328
00:11:08,293 --> 00:11:08,793
home.

329
00:11:08,794 --> 00:11:09,294
If you drive, that line is yours —

330
00:11:09,295 --> 00:11:09,795
and most people go their whole lives never

331
00:11:09,796 --> 00:11:10,296
knowing exactly where it falls.

332
00:11:10,297 --> 00:11:10,797
If knowing where your rights actually stop

333
00:11:10,798 --> 00:11:11,298
changed how you'll see the next traffic

334
00:11:11,299 --> 00:11:11,799
stop, hit like —

335
00:11:11,800 --> 00:11:12,300
it honestly helps more drivers find this.

336
00:11:12,301 --> 00:11:12,801
And tell me in the comments: were you

337
00:11:12,802 --> 00:11:13,302
taught that police need a warrant to search

338
00:11:13,303 --> 00:11:13,803
your car?

339
00:11:13,804 --> 00:11:14,304
Because for a hundred years,

340
00:11:14,305 --> 00:11:14,805
they haven't.
